        "status": "success",
        "rationale": "The structural obligations in `requirements.sdk.graph.agent-calls.judge.phases` are the typestate design. Each is backed by the supplied roots.\n\n- \"An ask builder begins in `ContextPhase`\": `crates/trust-but-verify/src/procedure.rs#impl[ValidationContext<'d, D>]#3/fn[ask]` returns `Ask<'_, '_, D, R, ContextPhase>`. A builder that started in another state would break this.\n- \"Only that state exposes `context`\": `context` is defined only in `impl[Ask<'a, 'd, D, R, ContextPhase>]`, which is a root. The only other `fn context` in the crate is an unrelated one in the markdown outline builder. The generic `impl<..., P>` blocks have no `context` method, so the method really is limited to that phase. A single generic `context` on all phases would give the same runtime results but would violate the text.\n- \"The first call to `contents_of_current_node`, `contents_of_node`, or `contents_of_successors` enters `NodePhase`\": each of these three roots returns `Ask<..., NodePhase>`. Each pushes an `Insertion` and then calls `into_node_phase`. `into_node_phase` rebuilds the `Ask` with `PhantomData` as `NodePhase`, and it is reached from the marked roots. `contents_of_successors` does this for an empty successor selection too, because the phase change does not depend on the selection.\n- \"allows further node insertions and awaiting\": the `contents_of_*` methods live in the generic `impl<P>` block, so they remain available in `NodePhase`. `await_phase!(NodePhase)` provides the `IntoFuture` implementation for that phase.\n- \"with no `context` method or transition back\": `NodePhase` has no `context` method, and nothing converts back to `ContextPhase`. The `NodePhase` doc compile_fail examples and its `{test}` marker give the compile-time check.\n\nThe `Ask` struct and the generic `impl<P>` helpers are reached from the marked roots, so they need no marker of their own. The `agent_context` citation is context only. I found no unmapped structural obligation."
      }
